;; talk/trader--structure.scm — structure lens on surfaces/trader.scm.

(objection 'o-trader-structure-1 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'trader-ticket :lens "structure"
  :claim (protocol "core/account/utils.ts" "The ticket shows can-send, which the protocol defines as my collateral share plus what they owe me plus unused credit granted to me, minus holds, and says part of it may be borrowed. That is the blended trading number the same file refuses in d-trader-refuses and the person's own limits list. The decision forbids the blend and the screen that carries the order shows it; the trader sizes the order from room the hub lent them and reads it as their own money. The same figure sits on trader-home. No decision chooses to show it, and none allows it."))

(objection 'o-trader-structure-2 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'trader-home :lens "structure"
  :claim (guess "The first screen offers four flows (limits, pull, move, leave) and not the trade flow, and no screen offers trader-ticket, trader-orders or trader-fills. Their only route in is undeclared navigation. The first-screen decision settles what is shown first, but the structure also settles that a session opens on screens the trader visits rarely while the job they came for, trading at a price, sits behind a path nobody decided. The decision's own changes-if is an observation of five traders, yet its :decider is 'user, so the question that only watching can answer is assigned to someone who cannot answer it."))

(objection 'o-trader-structure-3 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'd-trader-who :lens "structure"
  :claim (protocol "core/entity/tx/handlers/system/basic.ts" "The leaning is argued only against the desk seat, through the board threshold. Nothing is said for own-book over bot-supervisor, though the file then builds a gap, a need and an app around a program trading for the person. The person's :is also includes money a desk put under their name, which the leaning excludes. The cost is that the surface is written for someone who is not the person it defines, and the third reading, which several later nodes quietly assume, was never weighed."))

(objection 'o-trader-structure-4 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'd-trader-hub-limits :lens "structure"
  :claim (protocol "core/account/tx/handlers/swap/resolve/settlement.ts" "One decision bundles three separate choices: how far the hub may owe me, when to top up collateral, and the most fee for that. They have different risk holders and different evidence. The options are also uneven: inherit is defined as the protocol defaults that the :because says must not be inherited, and per-order is the question-per-order the person cannot afford, so only one option survives the file's own text. Meanwhile trader-default-credit-to-hub sets credit to 0 with risk on nobody, though the cited rule says zero credit lets the trader receive only what the hub has locked, so the cost lands on the trader as refused fills until the setup is done."))

(objection 'o-trader-structure-5 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'trader-default-silent-after :lens "structure"
  :claim (guess "Ten seconds and a 2% price distance (trader-default-price-distance) are numbers that decide when orders stop and when the trader is asked. Both are made as defaults, with no decision, no options and no changes-if. d-trader-silent-hub picks the response but not the threshold, and its own changes-if talks about outages of an hour, which a ten second rule will fire on far more often. A false stop blocks every new order to a working hub in a fast market; a late one leaves the trader trading into a dead hub. The default also starts an automatic halt that the decision describes as a question to the trader."))

(objection 'o-trader-structure-6 :by "sonnet-structure" :to 'd-trader-move-money :lens "structure"
  :claim (canon 'htlc-constants-are-simnet "Both-shown is presented as a design choice, but on an EVM mainnet routed payments are not allowed until expiry values are decided, so at launch there the only path is the two-chain-wait one, which fails the job as the person states it (minutes, not tomorrow). The routed-only and through-chain-only options therefore describe different launches, not different designs, and the real decisions, which path is the default where both exist and what the trader is told where only the slow one exists, are not made. The flow also bundles both paths under one :ends, though one returns the money on failure and the other leaves it between hubs."))
